<%# ── TESTIMONIALS (any copy) ────────────────────────────────────── %>
<%
  /* Count items with actual review text, not just items.
     The guard here used to be `_d.items.length`, which asks whether the
     array exists — not whether anything is in it. On 2026-09-13 production
     was carrying three testimonial entries with empty text, empty author
     and empty location, so the homepage rendered three blank review cards
     under "What Our Customers Say": a bare pair of quote marks, no name,
     and five gold stars (the star loop uses `ti.rating || 5`, so it draws
     a full five-star rating for a review that does not exist).

     Nothing errored. An SEO crawler found it first, reporting three empty
     <strong> tags — which was the smallest symptom of the problem, not the
     problem. A customer would have seen three hollow reviews on a page
     asking them to spend thousands of dollars.

     So: an entry with no review text is not a review, and the section with
     no reviews is not a section. Both are now skipped. If the testimonials
     ever empty out again the section disappears cleanly instead of
     rendering its own skeleton. */
  var _testItems = ((_baseKey === 'testimonials' && _d.items) || [])
                     .filter(function(ti){ return ti && String(ti.text || '').trim(); });
%>
<% if (_baseKey === 'testimonials' && _d.enabled !== false && _testItems.length) { %>
